                        "form_id": "fca3f8e0-27be-4506-9cb4-77553ec8b0ba",
                        "entry_id": "4615b321-e545-30d3-8e5f-7a1367ff3da2",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "salaams"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                        "entry_id": "4615b321-e545-30d3-8e5f-7a1367ff3da2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A low bow as a ceremonial act of deference."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee0f525f-fe99-4e6c-842f-41c80ff2efe7",
                                "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                                "text": "1840, John Wilson, “On the Genius and Character of Burns” in John Wilson and Robert Chambers, The Land of Burns, Glasgow: Blackie & Son, Volume 2, p. lxxv,\nFinally, Josiah might have made his salaam to the Exciseman just as he was folding up that letter […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "33e8caf5-14c0-496c-95be-34abf781c866",
                                "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                                "text": "[…] the finest dressed one entered first; put her two hands to her forehead, then to her breasts, and with her two hands touched the Colonel’s foot; this is called a grand salam […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4b4b33d5-4dd7-4e30-8544-c380a5dfb6e2",
                                "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                                "text": "1895, Rabindranath Tagore, letter dated 14 August, 1895 in Glimpses of Bengal, London: Macmillan, 1921, pp. 160-161,\nMy servant was late one morning, and I was greatly annoyed at his delay. He came up and stood before me with his usual salaam, and with a slight catch in his voice explained that his eight-year-old daughter had died last night."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "747c22e4-f9a8-4cdc-92e3-1fb1cb4a5dce",
                                "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                                "text": "The old gentleman made several deep salaams."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "312f5451-3580-4162-80c8-a52a4237fb1e",
                                "sense_id": "ad3d35c6-08ba-3f0b-9068-429d4c770aae",
                                "text": "Mr. Tsoutsou and his wife appeared for just a moment to see how I was faring, commented bravely on the delicious, appetizing appearance of the skinned fish and disappeared with bows and salaams which sent an electric thrill through the assembled patrons of Herakleion’s most distinguished restaurant."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                "word": "salaam",
                "pos": "verb",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "dd672b96-1bd6-4626-a208-0718363fa9b8",
                        "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                        "tags": [
                            "present",
                            "singular",
                            "third-person"
                        ],
                        "form": "salaams"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "5a2f267b-011a-4d0a-b82d-e310b8c9e9e4",
                        "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "present"
                        ],
                        "form": "salaaming"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "7669a8ee-6289-4c51-90e3-779847d10bfd",
                        "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "salaamed"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "8205fac6-c6b3-47c3-9003-de2ecf80a45b",
                        "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "salaamed"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                        "entry_id": "a38d9f21-2be3-3983-a6e8-35fac8faba1f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To perform a salaam to (someone)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b9074080-66d4-4a3c-9840-931c70ad8590",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "[H]e went away salaaming, and protesting his friendship and his master’s goodwill."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "df640c63-b730-4e66-9feb-e7d9bdb6bedf",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "He salaamed low to Flory, covering his face with his hand […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "724c8e47-a765-47a7-8489-1483572e8edd",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "Outside the town, three ragged children salaamed the Governor from the back of a camel."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c29dbac6-2f5d-4606-804c-b4b8d330716f",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "Beside himself, his possibilities; / salaaming hours of a half-blind morning / while the rainy lepers salaamed back,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d96e8622-5f96-4154-82c2-336c2a1160c7",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "The tea-planters’ houses of Ceylon […] were often surrounded by admirable lawns of coarse mountain grass, upon which the planters’ ladies enviably sat, buzzed about by harmless insects and salaamed by passing serfs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "01cbbb58-0f9a-409e-af53-da3cbf966d3a",
                                "sense_id": "f5f19c9b-7db2-32db-a624-f37aebfe0bf4",
                                "text": "Nantwich made a kind of diving or salaaming motion with his hands, and the man nodded and smiled."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            }
        ],
